    <h1 class="sr-spoke-header-title">The Colonial Encounter</h1>
    <p class="sr-spoke-header-deck">How Britain took Nigeria — the Berlin Conference, indirect rule, and the catastrophe of the warrant chiefs.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sr-spoke-content">

    <p class="sr-lede">The colonial forces that appear in <em>Things Fall Apart</em> were the product of specific European decisions, specific administrative strategies, and specific failures. This section covers that history: the Berlin Conference that partitioned Africa, the British strategy of indirect rule, the catastrophic warrant chief system, and the missionaries whose gentle approach proved most disruptive of all.</p>

    <h2>The Berlin Conference, 1884–1885: Partitioning a Continent</h2>

    <p>By the 1880s, European powers were competing intensely for African territory — its raw materials, its land, its strategic position, and the commercial routes to which it offered access. To prevent open conflict between European rivals, German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck convened a conference in Berlin beginning November 15, 1884. Fourteen nations attended.</p>

    <p>No African representatives were present. No African leaders were consulted.</p>

    <ImageCaption
      src="/images/summer-reading/tfa/t3-berlin-conference.jpg"
      alt="Engraving of the Berlin Conference of 1884–85, showing European delegates seated around a large table"
      figureNumber={1}
      caption="The Berlin Conference, 1884–85. Fourteen European nations and the United States negotiated the partition of Africa. No African representative was present at the table."
      credit="Die Gartenlaube, 1884. Public domain."
      accent="tfa"
    />

    <p>The conference established the <strong>principle of effective occupation</strong>: a European power could claim African territory only if it could demonstrate actual administrative control, not merely coastal presence. This principle accelerated the Scramble for Africa, as colonial powers rushed to establish interior control before their rivals. By 1914, the conference participants and their successors had divided virtually all of Africa among themselves. Only Liberia and Ethiopia remained independent.</p>

    <p>Britain's position in the Nigeria region was formalized at Berlin. Through a combination of commercial enterprise (the Royal Niger Company, chartered in 1886) and military pressure, Britain had been extending its influence along the Niger River for decades. The Berlin Conference ratified this dominance and created the framework within which the Protectorate of Southern Nigeria would be formally established in 1900.</p>

    <p>The borders drawn at Berlin and in subsequent negotiations were entirely indifferent to the political, ethnic, and cultural organization of the peoples being divided. The Igbo, Hausa, Yoruba, and hundreds of other peoples with distinct languages, cultures, and governance systems were lumped into a single colonial entity whose borders reflected European negotiation, not African reality.</p>

    <h2>Frederick Lugard and the Doctrine of Indirect Rule</h2>

    <p>The British colonial strategy in Nigeria was built around the doctrine of <strong>indirect rule</strong>, developed by Frederick Lugard (1858–1945), who served as High Commissioner of Northern Nigeria from 1900 and as Governor-General of the amalgamated Nigeria from 1914 to 1919.</p>

    <p>The principle was economical: rather than deploying large numbers of expensive British administrators across a vast territory, rule through existing local authorities. Co-opt indigenous leaders, place British officials above them, and use the existing hierarchy to collect taxes, maintain order, and administer colonial law.</p>

    <p><strong>Indirect rule in northern Nigeria</strong> was relatively effective. The Hausa and Fulani of the north had large, hierarchically organized Islamic emirates with established rulers — emirs — who could be co-opted into the colonial administrative framework. The British placed themselves at the top of an existing hierarchy without having to rebuild the entire structure.</p>

    <ImageCaption
      src="/images/summer-reading/tfa/t1-lugard-sokoto.jpg"
      alt="The Sultan of Sokoto and the emirs of Gwandu and Kano at the London Zoological Gardens with Frederick Lugard"
      figureNumber={2}
      caption="The Sultan of Sokoto and the emirs of Gwandu and Kano with Frederick Lugard at the London Zoological Gardens. Indirect rule in the Islamic north succeeded by co-opting existing hierarchical rulers — a strategy that had no equivalent in Igboland's acephalous governance structure."
      credit="Wikimedia Commons. Public domain."
      accent="tfa"
    />

    <p><strong>Indirect rule in Igboland was a catastrophe.</strong> The Igbo had no emirs. They had no kings, no paramount chiefs, no permanent rulers with authority over more than a single village. When the British arrived looking for chiefs to co-opt, they found village councils, age grades, and the egwugwu — institutions that did not fit the administrative model they needed.</p>

    <p>Their solution was the <strong>warrant chief system</strong>: they selected individuals — sometimes arbitrarily, often rewarding those most willing to collaborate — and issued them written warrants granting colonial authority over their communities. These warrant chiefs were frequently men with no traditional standing, which made them resented by the communities they were supposed to govern. Without traditional legitimacy, they depended entirely on British backing to maintain their authority; they frequently used that backing to enrich themselves and settle personal scores. The system was corrupt by design.</p>

    <p>The warrant chiefs collected taxes, enforced court decisions, and administered colonial law, but they did so without any of the accountability mechanisms that traditional Igbo governance had built over generations. The village council could challenge a decision; the egwugwu could override a ruling; the community could refuse consensus on a policy it opposed. Under the warrant chief system, none of these mechanisms remained. The British had replaced Igbo governance with a distorted simulation of it.</p>

    <h2>The Women's War of 1929</h2>

    <p>The most dramatic demonstration of the warrant chief system's failure came in the Women's War (<em>Ogu Umunwanyi</em>) of 1929.</p>

    <p>When rumors spread that the British intended to extend direct taxation to women — adding to taxes already imposed on men — Igbo and Ibibio women mobilized through traditional women's associations that the colonial administration had entirely failed to account for or understand. Tens of thousands of women across a wide area marched to Native Court buildings and demanded the resignation of the warrant chiefs. They danced, sang protest songs, and wore ferns and vines (the traditional markers of women's collective action). When the British responded with force, thirty-two women were killed.</p>

    <p>The uprising exposed not only the warrant chief system but the entire administrative assumption behind indirect rule in Igboland: that a handful of appointed men could represent and govern communities whose actual decision-making was distributed across councils, age grades, and gender-specific institutions, an intricate system about which the British had not bothered to learn.</p>

    <p>In the aftermath of the Women's War, the British conducted a formal inquiry, acknowledged the failures of the warrant chief system, and began to study Igbo governance seriously for the first time. The system was eventually dismantled and replaced with a structure more attentive to actual Igbo political organization.</p>

    <p><em>This event postdates the novel's setting</em>, but it illustrates the kind of organized resistance that Okonkwo, in the novel's final section, fails to inspire.</p>

    <h2>The Missionaries: Disruption Through Respect</h2>

    <p>The missionaries who arrive in <em>Things Fall Apart</em> are not uniformly brutal or contemptuous. Mr. Brown, the first missionary to establish a lasting church in Umuofia, is patient, genuinely curious, and willing to engage seriously with Igbo theology. He learns the language. He sits with the elder Akunna for long philosophical conversations. He treats Igbo religion with intellectual respect.</p>

    <p>He is also the most structurally effective agent of colonial disruption in the novel.</p>

    <p>The Church Missionary Society (CMS) and the Roman Catholic Mission (RCM) were the two primary missionary organizations in Nigeria. Both arrived ahead of formal colonial administration in many areas, establishing schools and congregations before district commissioners appeared. Mission schools became the primary pathway to Western literacy and education, which quickly became the primary pathway to employment in the colonial administration. The missionaries were not simply converting souls; they were producing the administrative class that would govern colonial Nigeria.</p>

    <p><strong>What the church actually disrupted</strong> was not simply Igbo religious belief in the abstract. It also dismantled the institutions through which Igbo social, political, and judicial life functioned.</p>

    <p>The <strong>egwugwu courts</strong> lost their authority as colonial courts replaced traditional jurisdiction. When a dispute went to the District Commissioner's court rather than to the egwugwu, the accumulated legitimacy of generations of Igbo jurisprudence was replaced by the rulings of an alien legal system.</p>

    <p>The <strong>Oracle's</strong> sanction for war and collective action was rendered irrelevant by colonial prohibition on inter-village conflict. Umuofia's famous fighting strength — the source of its regional authority — was neutralized.</p>

    <p>The <strong>village council's</strong> authority to exile, punish, and enforce communal decisions was superseded by the Native Court, which recognized warrant chiefs rather than elders.</p>

    <p>The <strong>social fabric</strong> binding Umuofia was split between converts and non-converts, creating internal divisions that the community's consensus-based governance could not easily bridge.</p>

