    <p><strong>Why the church attracted converts:</strong> The missionaries specifically targeted the margins of Igbo society — the <em>osu</em> (outcasts), the mothers of twins, those who had suffered most under Igbo hierarchical norms. The church offered them dignity, inclusion, and a community outside the system that had excluded them. Nwoye, who cannot meet his father's demands for masculine achievement, finds in the church a different set of values and a different community. His conversion is not simply religious; it is an escape from a structure within which he cannot succeed.</p>

    <p>This is one of Achebe's most uncomfortable arguments: colonialism succeeded partly because the culture it displaced had its own exclusions, and some of the people most desperately seeking escape from those exclusions were willing to accept the alternative the missionaries offered. The church exploited not only Igbo culture's strengths but also its genuine failures.</p>

    <h2>The District Commissioner: The Colonial Gaze</h2>

    <p>The District Commissioner who appears in the novel's final section — the representative of formal British administrative authority — is not cruel in any obvious personal sense. He considers himself a reasonable man, engaged in a civilizing project. He has spent years bringing "civilization to different parts of Africa." He plans to write a book about it.</p>

    <p>His book's title — <em>The Pacification of the Primitive Tribes of the Lower Niger</em> — is Achebe's summary of the self-image of the British colonial project. The title encodes every assumption the novel has spent two hundred pages dismantling: that the Igbo were primitive, that what happened to them was pacification of "savage" instincts, and that the story belongs to the man with the pen rather than to the people whose world was destroyed.</p>

    <p>The District Commissioner will devote "a reasonable paragraph" to Okonkwo — a man whose life, whose world, whose tragedy the reader has followed across the entire novel. That reduction — from a fully realized human life to a paragraph in a colonial administrator's memoir — is Achebe's final, most devastating statement about the impact of colonialism on Igbo history.</p>

